0}], "id": 0}], "type": "DWS", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": " Let's write about high availability!"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Take Away"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}, {"type": "text", "content": ""}], "correct_feedback": "Correct!", "incorrect_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "Whoops! Something important is missing."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Maybe next time..."}], "allowed_attempts": 2}}, {"contents": [{"type": "skill", "content": "Reading"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Writing"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Speaking"}], "type": "learning_skills"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Instructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self-Assessment."}], "type": "post_instructions"}], "id": 11}], "stimuli": [{"_data": [], "id": 0, "contents": [{}], "type": "welcome", "template_override": 1}, {"template_override": 1, "title": "", "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe7n7le03ob11re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<div align=\"justify\"><span class=\"strong\"> Scott: </span> Lisa, do you know what high availability is?<br/>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Lisa: </span> Of course. It is the possibility of using any online system  24/7 from anywhere; that system is online even when the system presents problems.<br/>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Scott:  </span>  So it is full-time available even with problems or failures happening around. But how can a system be kept online all the time?<br/>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Lisa: </span> There are products and features to cover downtime, even if it is planned or not. For example, they can use Oracle's real application clusters, which allows Oracle database to run on a set of clustered servers; if one fails, the system will work with the remaining ones.<br/>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Scott: </span> Wait a minute. I've heard of Oracle Data Guard, is it one of those programs for covering downtime? <br/>"}, {"time": "53000", "image": "enpe7n7le03ob11re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<span class=\"strong\"> Lisa: </span> Yes. That one creates, manages and monitors databases that are on standby, so they can survive failures or data corruption. Those standby databases are basically consistent copies. Oracle Streams is another example, it sends and manages data transactions in a data stream, and you can decide what information is put into the stream. You can use Online Table Redefinition as well; it will reorganize scripts and tables, even while used by the client.<br/>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Scott: </span> There are too many tools!<br/>"}, {"time": "88500", "image": "enpe7n7le03ob11re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<span class=\"strong\"> Lisa: </span> Just understand that Oracle Database has many ways to protect the data. One of the last examples would be Automatic Store management. This one creates disk groups, which have a set of disks within to make grouping and information management easier. Finally, the Flash Recovery Area. It is a unified storage location for all recovery-related files that generates copies of files into disk groups.<br/>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Scott: </span> It seems they are all related to one another!<br/>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Lisa: </span> Indeed. That will allow the system to be available all the time: it's not called high availability just because.</div>"}], "coverimg": "enpe7n7le03ob11re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n7le03ob11re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}]}], "type": "scholar"}]}